{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310740,"nonce":"SVh7+50vylIjYwKWZj2hEW7uClI=","title":"Ptr\u003cT\u003e.deref() is broken for EVERY T: Ptr\u003cInt\u003e and Ptr\u003cSingle\u003e return 0, Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e returns the raw bit pattern. write() works. Use ...","message":"**VERIFIED 2026-08-21 at 7c5c552b.** Not fixed — do not build on `Ptr\u003cT\u003e`.\n\n```haxe\nMem.storeF64(base + Usize.fromInt(24), 2.5);\nMem.loadF64(...)                     -\u003e 2.5          correct\n(Ptr.fromRaw(base):Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e).offset(3).deref()  -\u003e 4612811918334230528   BITS\n(Ptr.fromRaw(base):Ptr\u003cSingle\u003e).offset(3).deref() -\u003e 0\n(Ptr.fromRaw(base):Ptr\u003cInt\u003e).offset(3).deref()    -\u003e 0\n```\n\n`write()` IS correct — a `Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e.write(7.25)` is visible to `Mem.loadF64`.\nOnly the read is wrong, and it is wrong for every element type, so this is not\nthe missing-F64-suffix problem it first looks like.\n\n**WHAT I TRIED AND REVERTED:** the size-typed dispatch at\n`compiler/src/ir/mir/expr/call/stdlib_runtime.rs` (~:1400) maps\n`F32 =\u003e \"_4f\"`, `I32 =\u003e \"_4\"`, `U8/I8/Bool =\u003e \"_1\"`, and **F64 falls to the\nsize-erased default that loads an i64** — which explains `Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e` exactly.\nAdding `Ptr_deref_8f`/`Ptr_write_8f`/`Ptr_offset_8f` (mirroring the F32 trio in\n`compiler/src/stdlib/systems.rs:37-42`) plus an `IrType::F64 =\u003e \"_8f\"` arm\nCHANGED NOTHING — reverted. `Ptr\u003cInt\u003e`/`Ptr\u003cSingle\u003e` returning 0 proves the\ntyped path is not reached at all, so the suffix table is not the defect.\nDiagnose where `Ptr_deref` actually lowers before touching the table again.\n\n**WHY IT WAS DEPRIORITISED:** the reason to care was that `Ptr\u003cT\u003e` is the\nnatural typed carrier that could emit GEPs. That rationale is GONE —\n[[reference_kernel_replacement_acceptance_bar]]'s companion work solved GEPs\ndirectly in the LLVM backend (4ba83731), so kernels get vectorised addresses\nfrom plain `Mem` arithmetic. `Ptr\u003cT\u003e` has no users in nue.\n\nSame family as the closure-capture bug (f81429b7) and `SIMD.set` (43bda3a2):\na float or a typed value read back through an integer-shaped path.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_typed_ptr_deref_broken` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}